{
  "id": "personalpronomen",
  "name": "Subject pronouns and the verb be",
  "level": "A1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "Subject pronouns stand for people and things: I, you, he, she, it, we, they. The verb be changes with them: I am, you are, she is. Use it for things, animals, weather and time.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "I am",
    "you are",
    "she is"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "I · you · he · she · it · we · they",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "am / is / are",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "rest of the sentence"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-verb\">She</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">is</span> a nurse. — <span class=\"vp-verb\">They</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">are</span> in Leeds. — <span class=\"vp-verb\">I</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">am</span> tired.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>Subject pronouns</strong> take the place of a name or a noun: <em>Amira is a nurse. <strong>She</strong> works in Leeds.</em> There are seven: <em>I, you, he, she, it, we, they</em>. Always write <strong>I</strong> with a capital letter.",
        "tag": "Subject pronouns"
      },
      {
        "html": "After the pronoun, <strong>be</strong> changes: <em>I <strong>am</strong> · you <strong>are</strong> · he / she / it <strong>is</strong> · we / you / they <strong>are</strong></em>. Short forms are normal in speech: <em>I'm, you're, he's, she's, it's, we're, they're</em>.",
        "tag": "am, is, are"
      },
      {
        "html": "For a <strong>negative</strong>, put <em>not</em> after be: <em>I am not, he is not, they are not</em>. Short forms: <em>I'm not, he isn't, they aren't</em>. For a <strong>question</strong>, put be first: <em>Are you ready? Is she a doctor?</em> There is no <em>amn't</em>.",
        "tag": "No and questions"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>You</strong> is one person or many people, and the verb is always <em>are</em>: <em>You are late, Ben. You are late, Ben and Sam.</em> <strong>They</strong> is for people and things, and also for one person when you do not say if it is a man or a woman: <em>Someone called. They left a message.</em>",
        "tag": "you and they"
      },
      {
        "html": "Use <strong>it</strong> for things, animals, weather and time: <em>My bag is red. It is new. It is cold today. It is six o'clock.</em> English sentences always need a subject: ✗ <em>Is cold.</em> ✓ <em>It is cold.</em>",
        "tag": "It"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>He are my friend.</em> → ✓ <em>He is my friend.</em> · ✗ <em>I is from Leeds.</em> → ✓ <em>I am from Leeds.</em> · ✗ <em>She no is here.</em> → ✓ <em>She isn't here.</em> Use <em>he / she</em> for people and <em>it</em> for things. Next: <a href=\"/object-pronouns\">object pronouns</a> (me, him, her, us, them), <a href=\"/present-simple\">the present simple</a> and <a href=\"/possessives\">my, your, his, her</a>.",
